  "articleBody": "Maintaining a log home is a bit different from a regular stick-built house, and there are some common missteps folks make. Here are a few to steer clear of:\n\n1. Relying on clear coats alone: They just don't offer enough protection from the elements. \n2. Waiting too long after water stops beading: That's your signal to re-stain. Don't put it off!\n3. Skipping inspections of high-exposure walls: Your south and west-facing walls take the most abuse, so give 'em extra attention.\n4. Not sealing log joints with chink or caulk: Open joints are an open invitation for water damage.\n5. Using generic exterior coatings: Log homes need special stains that are designed to let the wood breathe. Using the wrong product can cause big problems down the road.\n\nCatching these things early can save you a ton of hassle and money. If you're ever in doubt about the condition of your logs, or if you've got some serious peeling or rot, it's always a smart move to bring in a professional. We can assess what's going on and help you get things back on track.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
